# Plan Documents

`context/plan/<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>-<slug>.md` hold the plan for a piece of
work, derived from the related analysis. Plans are **living documents**: they are
updated during execution, not frozen. A plan exists to produce task files; a plan
without tasks has no execution value.

The plan carries the **"how"** — the implementation decisions worked out from the
analysis. Reference the analysis through `sources`; do not repeat its context,
evidence or options.

## Create

Create a new plan with the CLI (scaffolds the dated path and frontmatter):

```bash
sdt context new --type plan --title "<title>" [--objective <slug>] [--source <analysis-ref>]
```


When `--objective` is omitted and `--source` points to an analysis carrying an
`objective`, the plan inherits that slug.

## Intent first

A plan is required before implementation and derives from an analysis; the
analysis → plan → tasks → execution chain and its approval gates are defined in
AGENTS.md (HARD RULE 1). The plan is built on top of the analysis (set
`sources`); within a running plan, integrate/modify in place — do not
restart the chain.

**Subject resolution (`>plan`).** The `>plan` trigger is a **workflow** command
whose subject is an `analysis`. It resolves that analysis through the
working-context ladder (`context/commands/index.md`, *Working-context
resolution*): explicit payload → exactly one `status: active` analysis → the
analysis under discussion → **ask**. When more than one remains, list them and
ask; `updated` recency orders the list but never picks. Bare plan creation (no
source analysis) stays reachable via `sdt context new --type plan`.

**R2 — one analysis may feed several plans (waves).** The analysis/plan
relation is not 1:1: several plans may declare the same analysis in `sources`,
each covering a slice of it, and each is an independently shippable wave with
its own phases, its own final Validation phase and its own approval gate. To
keep a wave set navigable:

- the **analysis** carries the ordering in a `## Plans` section: one line per
  derived plan (number, slug, one-sentence scope, and the analysis objectives it
  covers) — the numbering lives there, never in filenames, so renaming a wave
  cannot break `links`;
- every sibling plan carries the **same `objective`** as its analysis, so
  `sdt context reindex` groups the set;
- each wave's plan states, in `## Objective`, which slice of the analysis it
  takes and which objectives it leaves to a sibling wave;
- a plan that would ship nothing independently is not a wave — fold it into the
  wave it belongs to.

## Workflow (plan → tasks → execution)

Follow strictly — the plan defines the work, the task files execute it:

1. **Create the plan** — write Objective, Constraints and assumptions, Out of
   scope, Phases (ending with a Validation phase), Verification and Completion
   criteria from the analysis, plus a
   **Design contract** (style, architecture and dependency choices — see
   `instructions/development.md`).
2. **Wait for plan approval** — stop after creating the plan and wait for
  explicit user approval before creating task files. Do not create task files
  as part of plan creation. The approval covers the plan as presented: a later
  or different stage needs its own approval (see AGENTS.md, *No silent
  downgrades*).
3. **Create the task file(s) after approval** — default to **one task file per
   plan** (`context/tasks/<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>-<slug-plan>.md`) holding one `##
   Phase <n>` section per phase; split into several files only to divide the
   work by context or across sub-agents (disjoint scopes, each with a stream
   label). See `instructions/tasks.md`. Link in both directions (plan frontmatter
   → task files, task file frontmatter → plan).
4. **Wait for task approval** — stop after creating the task files and wait for
  explicit user approval before implementation.
5. **Execute one task file at a time** — never work from the plan itself. Pick
   a task file with no pending dependencies, mark it **in progress** on
   take-in, complete items as they finish, then move to the next. The `>execute`
   trigger drives this step (subject: a plan and its task file; see
   `instructions/tasks.md` and the working-context ladder in
   `context/commands/index.md`). For work larger than one session, **claim**
   the task file before working it and pick from the `sdt context resume`
   frontier (see `tasks.md`, *Multi-session handoff*).
6. **Update in place** — plan and task files are **living**: reflect progress,
   decisions and deviations as they happen through the CLI (`sdt context check`
   for phase/checklist items, `sdt context touch` for a body-only edit), never
   by hand-editing `status`, `updated` or checkbox markers.

## Structure

```markdown
---
kind: plan
summary: "<1-2 sentence summary — MANDATORY, index source>"
context: "<objective / what triggered the plan>"
objective: <slug>         # optional: kebab-case group key shared with the
                          # analysis; inherited by this plan's task files
status: active            # active | completed | abandoned — statuses defined in the matrix (architecture/stack.md)
rigor: <P0-P4>            # optional: how much rigour this wave demands of itself
                          # (see "Rigour dial" below)
created: "<ISO 8601>"
updated: "<ISO 8601>"
links:
  - analysis/20260905-...-analysis-slug.md
  - tasks/<date>-<plan-slug>.md
project: <project>
agent: <agent/tool>          # optional
model: <model id>            # optional
session: <session id>        # optional
---

## Objective
## Constraints and assumptions
## Out of scope
## Design contract (style, architecture, dependencies)
## Phases (one task file by default, phases as sections)
## Dependency graph
## Completion criteria
## Review log
```

- **No H1 title** — body starts at H2; see AGENTS.md (document conventions).

## Section descriptions

- **Objective** — 1-3 sentences: what this plan achieves and why. Must be
  clear enough that someone reading only this section understands the goal.
- **Constraints and assumptions** — explicit limitations and assumptions. If an
  assumption is a guess (low confidence) and getting it wrong would be costly,
  resolve it before marking the plan approved instead of building tasks on it. A
  **data-format** assumption a migration or import depends on (a field's meaning,
  a serialization shape) is verified against a **real sample** before approval —
  never inferred from a field name; a mismatch found after scheduling is exactly
  the rework this rule prevents.
- **Out of scope** — work explicitly excluded, to prevent scope creep. Be
  specific: not just "other things" but named areas. State a schema boundary
  precisely: "no schema change" is ambiguous — say whether optional fields are in
  scope, so a consumer derived from the plan cannot read it the other way.
- **Design contract** — the style, architecture and dependency choices this
  plan commits to, agreed with the user before tasks are created (see
  `instructions/development.md`). Each non-trivial choice is stated here so
  execution never invents style, structure or libraries.
- **Phases** — each phase becomes a `## Phase <n>` section in the plan's task
  file (split files are opt-in, see `instructions/tasks.md`). Each phase has:
  **Goal** (one sentence), **Depends on** (none / Phase X), the **analysis
  objectives** it covers, and the **approach** — the implementation and the "how"
  (the design decisions, files and patterns). The executable checklist is **not**
  in the plan: the `- [ ]` steps live only in the task file's `## Phase <n>`
  section, so a phase and its task section never restate the same list.
  **Phase rules**
  (user-mandated):
  1. one phase = **one deliverable/concern**, stated in its Goal sentence;
  2. **no cap on the number of phases** — a plan may have any count;
  3. keep phases **small**: aim for ~5-7 checklist items; a >10-item phase
     section triggers a `sdt context lint` SUGGESTION to split the phase;
  4. a phase must be completable by a **single agent in one focused session**
     without re-reading the whole plan;
  5. when refining an existing phase, use alphanumeric sub-phase suffixes
     (`1a`, `1b`) instead of widening it;
  6. every plan ends with a dedicated final **Validation phase** that verifies
     the delivered changes (run the project's build/test/lint and
     `sdt context reindex`/`lint`) — it is always the last phase, even when a
     previous phase already ran checks. It **enumerates its render cells up
     front** (routes × viewports × states, or the equivalent surface matrix) and
     checks each; a partial pass names the cells it skipped rather than
     reporting a general pass;
   6a. it includes a **blind reader-test** cell when the deliverable is a
     document a reader meets cold: hand the finished document to a **fresh
     context** with no shared working state and ask it to report what it could
     not find, what was ambiguous, and what it had to assume. The author's own
     re-read cannot catch this — the author already knows what the document
     means. When no fresh context is available the cell is **deferred, not
     skipped**: record that the check did not run and which claim stays
     unverified;
  7. each phase names the **analysis objectives** it covers; the task file for
     that phase carries a `## Design` study of those objectives (see
     `instructions/tasks.md`).
  8. a generated artifact and its **registration** ship in the **same phase** —
     a template without its index row, or a new surface without the table that
     points to it, leaves the generated set inconsistent and the project's own
     verification fails on a change that looked complete.
- **From a requirements document to phases** — when the request arrives as a
  requirements document rather than an objective, decompose it into epics, then
  into phases, and state the dependency between them:
  - one phase delivers one deliverable; a phase that serves two epics is two
    phases;
  - name the **inter-phase dependency** where one exists (`Phase 3 needs the
    Phase 1 schema`); independent phases are declared independent, never left
    implicit;
  - **elicit the quality gates explicitly.** A requirements document states
    *what*, rarely *how it is judged good*; ask for the acceptance criteria before
    planning, or record in the plan that none were given and which ones you chose.
    An unstated gate is discovered at delivery, when it is expensive.
- When this does not apply: a small, direct objective with no requirements
  document skips the decomposition and goes straight to phases.
- **Good vs bad splitting** — bad: one "Implement feature" phase with 15 mixed
  items (schema + backend + CLI + tests in one file). Good: split into phases,
  each with a single deliverable and ≤ ~5 items: schema → backend → CLI → tests.
- **Dependency graph** — only needed when there are more than ~3 tasks or
  non-obvious ordering. Use a simple ASCII diagram.
- **Wave dependencies are explicit.** A plan/wave's `depends_on` names the plans
  it needs; an **absent** `depends_on` means **INDETERMINATE** (unknown), never
  "independent" — state `depends_on: []` when there is genuinely no dependency.
- **Completion criteria** — checkboxes for the global exit conditions (all
  phases completed — including the final Validation phase — full test suite
  green, etc.). State only what **this** plan delivers: a criterion that implies
  a sibling wave's scope, or an unverified chunk/graph split, over-claims —
  verify a claimed split against the real output before writing it as a
  criterion. **Reference-only model**: the plan's `## Phases` and this
  section state *intent*; the executed checklist lives in the task file, and a
  plan derives `completed` from its task files, not from these checkboxes. Tick
  a plan checklist item through the CLI (`sdt context check <plan> <id> --done`)
  only to record intent — never to gate completion.
- **Review log** — only if the plan is revised after approval. Not for execution
  progress (that lives in task files). Format: `- <YYYY-MM-DD>: <what changed and why>`.

## Rigour dial (optional)

`rigor` records how much rigour this wave demands of itself, so a cheap wave is
cheap **on purpose** and a dangerous one is labelled before it ships. It is
optional; absent means the plan makes no claim. The dial is about the shape of
the failure the work risks, not about effort or size:

| Dial | The work earns it when… |
|------|-------------------------|
| `P0` | the change is trivially reversible and its blast radius is one file |
| `P1` | the failure is visible in the ordinary workflow — a test, a lint, a failed command |
| `P2` | the failure surfaces only under an unusual input, load or ordering |
| `P3` | **the failure mode is invisible to its own author** — it passes review and still ships wrong |
| `P4` | the failure is silent, expensive and outside this project's ability to notice it |

P3 is the threshold that changes the Validation phase's obligations: when the
failure mode is invisible to the author, the validate step cannot be "the tests
pass" — it names the specific evidence that would have caught it, or records
that no such evidence exists. A plan that quietly ships P3 work as P1 is the
miscalibration this dial exists to prevent.

`rigor` is a per-plan claim, distinct from the per-phase validation contract: it
never lowers the gate, it states what the gate has to answer.

## Frontmatter properties

| Property | Mandatory | Description |
|----------|-----------|-------------|
| kind | yes | Document type, fixed value `plan`; index and lint depend on it. |
| summary | yes | 1-2 sentence summary → index source. |
| context | yes | Objective / what triggered the plan. |
| objective | no | Kebab-case group key shared with the analysis; analyses, plans and tasks group by it (task files inherit it). |
| status | yes | `active` / `completed` / `abandoned`. |
| rigor | no | `P0` / `P1` / `P2` / `P3` / `P4` — how much rigour this wave demands of itself, from the failure shape (see *Rigour dial*). Absent means no claim. |
| created | yes | ISO 8601 UTC creation date (use `sdt time iso`). |
| updated | yes | ISO 8601 UTC last-edit date, written only by the CLI; refresh a body-only edit with `sdt context touch`. |
| links | yes | Relative paths to source docs (analysis) and task files. |
| project | yes | Project id from `.sdt.yaml` (e.g. `sdt_01a2b3c4`). |
| agent | no | Agent/tool (AI or human) that created or last edited this file. |
| model | no | Model id of that agent (e.g. `opencode/big-pickle`) — provenance. |
| session | no | Session id for traceability across edits. |

## 5-stage development lifecycle

A plan is stage 2 of the 5-stage development lifecycle (Analysis → Plan → Tasks
per phase → Execution → Final reports) — the full cycle and its rules live in
AGENTS.md.

Plan and task files are updated during execution (not append-only); decisions and
`architecture/` follow their own rules (`instructions/decision.md`,
`instructions/architecture.md`).
